C = [
("h1", "4. The Test of Fulfilment"),
("p", "Claim: Moses gives Israel a second test, and it is a test of outcome. If a prophet speaks in the name of the LORD and the thing does not come to pass, the LORD did not speak it. The test settles the matter after the event, which is why the tests of source and loyalty carry the weight in the moment. Micaiah and Jeremiah each stake their office on it."),
("sub", "4.1 The two offences and the question"),
("p", "Deuteronomy 18 states the offence before it gives the test. “But the prophet, which shall presume to speak a word in my name, which I have not commanded him to speak, or that shall speak in the name of other gods, even that prophet shall die” (Deuteronomy 18:20). There are two offences: to speak in the LORD’s name what he did not command, and to speak in the name of other gods. The second is the offence of chapter 3. The first is the offence of chapter 2, a word that did not come from the LORD, with the prophet using his name for it."),
("p", "Then the people’s question, put in their own hearts: “And if thou say in thine heart, How shall we know the word which the LORD hath not spoken?” (Deuteronomy 18:21). The question is practical. Israel will hear prophets whose words differ, and she is told how to know the word the LORD has not spoken. The answer follows at once."),
("sub", "4.2 The test and what it can do"),
("p", "“When a prophet speaketh in the name of the LORD, if the thing follow not, nor come to pass, that is the thing which the LORD hath not spoken, but the prophet hath spoken it presumptuously: thou shalt not be afraid of him” (Deuteronomy 18:22). The test is stated carefully, and the care is worth noting. It speaks of a prophet who speaks “in the name of the LORD.” It speaks of “the thing”, an event or a state of affairs that the prophet announced. And it names the verdict: the LORD has not spoken it, the prophet spoke it presumptuously, and Israel need not be afraid of him."),
("p", "The test has one great strength. It can show that a word was not the LORD’s, with no other evidence. A prophet who announces that a thing will happen, and it does not, is shown by the event itself. Nobody has to read the prophet’s heart or weigh his conduct. The LORD himself has said what the event means."),
("p", "The test has one equally plain limit. It acts after the event. A man deciding whether to follow a prophet today cannot use it to decide today. That is the reason the volume puts the test of source (chapter 2) and the test of loyalty (chapter 3) in front of it. In the moment, Israel could ask where a word came from and where it led. The test of fulfilment could confirm the answer later. Chapter 3 showed the other side of the same fact. A fulfilled sign did not validate a word that led from the LORD (Deuteronomy 13:2, 3). The test of fulfilment can condemn a prophet, and it cannot by itself approve one."),
("sub", "4.3 Micaiah against four hundred"),
("p", "First Kings 22 is the longest case in Scripture of the tests at work. Ahab king of Israel and Jehoshaphat king of Judah plan to go up against Ramothgilead. Jehoshaphat asks for the word of the LORD: “Enquire, I pray thee, at the word of the LORD to day” (1 Kings 22:5). Ahab gathers about four hundred prophets, and they answer, “Go up; for the Lord shall deliver it into the hand of the king” (1 Kings 22:6). One of them, Zedekiah, makes horns of iron and says, “Thus saith the LORD, With these shalt thou push the Syrians, until thou have consumed them” (1 Kings 22:11). The four hundred speak in the LORD’s name, with a symbol, and with one voice."),
("p", "Jehoshaphat is not satisfied. “Is there not here a prophet of the LORD besides, that we might enquire of him?” (1 Kings 22:7). Ahab names Micaiah and gives his reason for not asking him: “I hate him; for he doth not prophesy good concerning me, but evil” (1 Kings 22:8). A messenger urges Micaiah to join the others: “the words of the prophets declare good unto the king with one mouth: let thy word, I pray thee, be like the word of one of them, and speak that which is good” (1 Kings 22:13). Micaiah answers on the ground of the first test: “As the LORD liveth, what the LORD saith unto me, that will I speak” (1 Kings 22:14)."),
("p", "His first words to the king repeat the four hundred’s: “Go, and prosper: for the LORD shall deliver it into the hand of the king” (1 Kings 22:15). The king does not take them as meant: “How many times shall I adjure thee that thou tell me nothing but that which is true in the name of the LORD?” (1 Kings 22:16). The text does not say how Micaiah spoke. It records that the king heard the answer as other than the truth, and that Micaiah then spoke the word he had been given: “I saw all Israel scattered upon the hills, as sheep that have not a shepherd” (1 Kings 22:17)."),
("sub", "4.4 The lying spirit"),
("p", "Micaiah then tells what he saw. “I saw the LORD sitting on his throne, and all the host of heaven standing by him on his right hand and on his left” (1 Kings 22:19). The LORD asks, “Who shall persuade Ahab, that he may go up and fall at Ramothgilead?” and a spirit answers, “I will go forth, and I will be a lying spirit in the mouth of all his prophets” (1 Kings 22:20, 22). The LORD sends it. Micaiah draws the conclusion for the king: “the LORD hath put a lying spirit in the mouth of all these thy prophets, and the LORD hath spoken evil concerning thee” (1 Kings 22:23)."),
("p", "The volume reads the passage as judgment on Ahab and not as a licence for the LORD to deceive the innocent. The king had said he hated the one prophet who spoke evil of him (1 Kings 22:8). He had refused the word Micaiah gave in verse 17: “Did I not tell thee that he would prophesy no good concerning me, but evil?” (1 Kings 22:18). The scene Micaiah describes explains why four hundred prophets spoke what they spoke. It does not make their word the LORD’s in the sense of chapter 2. The text itself says the opposite: the LORD “hath put” the spirit in their mouth to persuade a king who would hear only good. Diamond, in chapter 8, calls the spirit the king’s own folly. The volume holds that the text gives the LORD the act, and adds that the act falls on a man who had already shut his ears (see Prophetic Commentary Volume 2 for the same passage)."),
("sub", "4.5 The stake: “the LORD hath not spoken by me”"),
("p", "Ahab has Micaiah put in prison. Micaiah answers with a sentence that is the test of Deuteronomy 18:22 in his own mouth. “If thou return at all in peace, the LORD hath not spoken by me. And he said, Hearken, O people, every one of you” (1 Kings 22:28). He stakes his whole office on the event. If Ahab returns in peace, he is no prophet. And he calls the people as witnesses."),
("p", "The event follows. Ahab disguises himself, and “a certain man drew a bow at a venture, and smote the king of Israel between the joints of the harness” (1 Kings 22:34). The king died at evening, and the writer ends the scene by tying it to the word: “according unto the word of the LORD which he spake” (1 Kings 22:38). The test of fulfilment has done what Deuteronomy 18:22 said it would do. The four hundred are shown by the event to have spoken what the LORD had not spoken to them. Micaiah is shown to have spoken from the LORD. The event came after the decision, and the king and the people had to decide before it."),
("sub", "4.6 Jeremiah 28:8, 9: the rule for the prophet of peace"),
("p", "Jeremiah 28 states a related rule. Jeremiah answers Hananiah first with “Amen: the LORD do so” (Jeremiah 28:6). He wishes it were true. Then: “The prophets that have been before me and before thee of old prophesied both against many countries, and against great kingdoms, of war, and of evil, and of pestilence. The prophet which prophesieth of peace, when the word of the prophet shall come to pass, then shall the prophet be known, that the LORD hath truly sent him” (Jeremiah 28:8, 9)."),
("p", "The volume reads the rule as a burden of proof laid on one kind of prophet. The earlier prophets had spoken of war, evil and pestilence, which was the burden of the Law’s warnings. A prophet who comes against that stream with a word of peace must wait for the event to be known as sent. The verse does not say that every prophet of peace is false. It does not say that a fulfilled word of peace validates a prophet who also teaches rebellion; chapter 3 closed that door. It says that the one who stands against the line of earlier prophets cannot be known by the hearers until the word comes to pass. That is a rule of the test of fulfilment, and it agrees with Deuteronomy 18:22."),
("ctbl", "The test of fulfilment in three passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What is announced", "What follows"),
    ("Deuteronomy 18:20–22", "A word spoken in the LORD’s name", "If the thing follow not, the LORD has not spoken it; do not fear the prophet"),
    ("1 Kings 22", "Ahab will prosper (the four hundred); Ahab will fall (Micaiah)", "Ahab is struck by a bow drawn at a venture and dies at even (22:34–38)"),
    ("Jeremiah 28:8, 9", "Peace, against the line of prophets who spoke of war and evil", "The prophet of peace is known when the word comes to pass"),
    ("What the test cannot do", "Decide a case before the event; approve a word that leads from the LORD", "The tests of source and loyalty carry the case in the moment"),
  ], [0.26, 0.38, 0.36]),
("sub", "4.7 The vision that tarries"),
("p", "The test of fulfilment can be misused, and the prophets say so. If a word that has not yet come to pass is called false, a patient hearer is punished for patience. Habakkuk is told the opposite. “Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it. For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry” (Habakkuk 2:2, 3). The LORD does not deny that the word has a long road. He says it has an appointed time, and that it speaks at the end."),
("p", "Ezekiel meets the opposite error in his own day. The people have a proverb: “The days are prolonged, and every vision faileth” (Ezekiel 12:22). It is the mockery of the hearer who has waited and decided the prophets were wrong. The LORD’s answer is to end the proverb: “I will make this proverb to cease … The days are at hand, and the effect of every vision” (Ezekiel 12:23). Then he states the principle: “For I am the LORD: I will speak, and the word that I shall speak shall come to pass; it shall be no more prolonged” (Ezekiel 12:25)."),
("p", "Put Habakkuk 2 beside Ezekiel 12 and the volume’s position on the test of fulfilment sharpens. The test in Deuteronomy 18:22 concerns a word spoken “in the name of the LORD” that “follow not, nor come to pass.” It is the failure of a specific, announced event that shows the LORD has not spoken. It does not license a hearer to declare a word dead because it has not yet come. Habakkuk says the vision tarries and Ezekiel says the mockers were wrong. A word with an appointed time that has not arrived has not failed."),
("p", "This is the reason the test carries the least weight in the moment. The hearer who must decide cannot yet see the outcome, and a word that is true may still be waiting. The tests of source and loyalty are what he can apply now. The test of fulfilment is how the LORD answers, in time, the question the hearer could not settle at the start. It confirms or condemns afterward; it does not tell the hearer what to do beforehand."),
("sub", "4.8 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "An announced event that does not come to pass shows that the LORD did not speak the word; the people need not fear the prophet.",
    "The test acts after the event; the tests of source and loyalty carry the weight when the decision must be made.",
    "Micaiah stakes his office on the outcome and is vindicated by an arrow drawn at a venture.",
    "The lying spirit is the LORD’s judgment on a king who would hear only good.",
    "Jeremiah 28:9 lays a burden of proof on the prophet who stands against the line of earlier prophets; it does not turn fulfilment into approval of a word that leads from the LORD.",
    "Habakkuk 2:2, 3 and Ezekiel 12:21–25 show that a word may tarry without failing; the test of fulfilment condemns a word that did not come to pass at its appointed time, and does not condemn patience.",
  ]),
]
